WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.211
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.511 --> 00:00:08.677
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:17.782
Unit forty-eight: Inequality. In this unit, you will discuss causes and solutions to economic inequality.

4
00:00:18.783 --> 00:00:19.866
wealth gap.

5
00:00:20.566 --> 00:00:24.910
wealth gap means the difference in wealth between rich and poor.

6
00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.999
The wealth gap has widened in many countries.

7
00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:31.839
Your turn. Say: wealth gap.

8
00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:35.529
wealth gap.

9
00:00:36.517 --> 00:00:38.052
income inequality.

10
00:00:38.752 --> 00:00:42.876
income inequality means unequal distribution of earnings.

11
00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:47.962
Income inequality affects health and education.

12
00:00:48.662 --> 00:00:52.150
Your turn. Say: income inequality.

13
00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:56.339
income inequality.

14
00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:58.676
social mobility.

15
00:00:59.376 --> 00:01:04.325
social mobility means the ability to move to a different social or economic position.

16
00:01:04.625 --> 00:01:07.309
Education increases social mobility.

17
00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:11.637
Your turn. Say: social mobility.

18
00:01:14.337 --> 00:01:15.814
social mobility.

19
00:01:16.817 --> 00:01:18.294
Gini coefficient.

20
00:01:18.994 --> 00:01:23.548
Gini coefficient means a number from 0 to 1 that measures inequality.

21
00:01:23.848 --> 00:01:27.078
A higher Gini coefficient means more inequality.

22
00:01:27.778 --> 00:01:31.022
Your turn. Say: Gini coefficient.

23
00:01:33.722 --> 00:01:35.048
Gini coefficient.

24
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.817
structural inequality.

25
00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:43.327
structural inequality means unfairness built into a society's systems.

26
00:01:43.627 --> 00:01:47.484
Structural inequality limits opportunities for rural children.

27
00:01:48.184 --> 00:01:52.380
Your turn. Say: structural inequality.

28
00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.755
structural inequality.

29
00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:58.751
privilege.

30
00:01:59.451 --> 00:02:02.960
privilege means advantages that some people have and others don't.

31
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.780
Recognising privilege is the first step to fairness.

32
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.284
Your turn. Say: privilege.

33
00:02:12.984 --> 00:02:13.822
privilege.

34
00:02:14.817 --> 00:02:16.120
redistribution.

35
00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:22.372
redistribution means sharing wealth more evenly through taxes and benefits.

36
00:02:22.672 --> 00:02:25.914
Redistribution funds schools and hospitals.

37
00:02:26.614 --> 00:02:30.090
Your turn. Say: redistribution.

38
00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.058
redistribution.

39
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:36.643
progressive taxation.

40
00:02:37.343 --> 00:02:42.257
progressive taxation means a system where richer people pay a higher tax rate.

41
00:02:42.557 --> 00:02:45.636
Progressive taxation is common in Europe.

42
00:02:46.336 --> 00:02:50.289
Your turn. Say: progressive taxation.

43
00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:54.477
progressive taxation.

44
00:02:55.483 --> 00:02:57.703
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:58.003 --> 00:03:02.615
Ms. Sarah, is inequality really a problem if everyone is getting richer?

46
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:09.271
Many economists insist that it be taken seriously, even in growing economies.

47
00:03:10.583 --> 00:03:11.329
Why?

48
00:03:12.617 --> 00:03:18.775
High inequality reduces social mobility. Children of poor families find it harder to move up.

49
00:03:20.083 --> 00:03:21.665
So what should governments do?

50
00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:28.258
Some propose that taxation be made more progressive, and that the money fund education.

51
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.550
Wouldn't that discourage investment?

52
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.987
That's the counter-argument. It is vital that policies balance fairness and growth.

53
00:03:39.283 --> 00:03:43.663
Grammar focus: Subjunctive and formal structures (mastery).

54
00:03:43.963 --> 00:03:47.936
The passive subjunctive is common in formal proposals.

55
00:03:48.236 --> 00:03:55.033
Economists insist that inequality be taken seriously. They propose that taxation be made more progressive.

56
00:03:55.333 --> 00:03:58.424
It is vital that policies balance fairness and growth.

57
00:03:58.724 --> 00:04:05.635
The passive subjunctive (that it be done) is typical of formal proposals and policy documents.

58
00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.651
Now read and listen. The Shape of Inequality.

59
00:04:09.951 --> 00:04:16.745
Over recent decades, global poverty has fallen, yet inequality within many countries has risen.

60
00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:23.858
Economists measure income inequality using tools such as the Gini coefficient.

61
00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:31.027
High inequality is associated with lower social mobility, poorer health and weaker trust.

62
00:04:32.317 --> 00:04:39.204
Structural inequality, such as unequal access to quality schools, can persist across generations.

63
00:04:40.517 --> 00:04:46.313
Some economists propose that taxation be made more progressive to fund public services.

64
00:04:47.617 --> 00:04:53.262
Others warn that excessive redistribution may discourage investment and innovation.

65
00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:59.498
Most agree, however, that equal opportunity in education is essential.

66
00:05:00.783 --> 00:05:04.559
It is imperative that growth benefit the many, not only the few.

67
00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.444
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:08.744 --> 00:05:12.485
The ability to move to a different social position is blank.

69
00:05:12.785 --> 00:05:19.330
A, social mobility. B, privilege. C, Gini coefficient.

70
00:05:23.317 --> 00:05:26.175
The answer is A: social mobility.

71
00:05:27.683 --> 00:05:31.053
The committee recommends that the law blank changed.

72
00:05:31.353 --> 00:05:34.356
A, is. B, be. C, was.

73
00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.117
The answer is B: be.

74
00:05:41.617 --> 00:05:45.149
It is imperative that every child blank access to school.

75
00:05:45.449 --> 00:05:48.545
A, has. B, have. C, had.

76
00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.979
The answer is B: have.

77
00:05:56.483 --> 00:06:00.108
A tax system where richer people pay a higher rate is blank.

78
00:06:00.408 --> 00:06:05.304
A, redistribution. B, progressive taxation. C, wealth gap.

79
00:06:09.317 --> 00:06:12.024
The answer is B: progressive taxation.

80
00:06:13.517 --> 00:06:17.455
According to the text, what is high inequality associated with?

81
00:06:17.755 --> 00:06:23.452
A, higher trust. B, lower social mobility. C, better health.

82
00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.785
The answer is B: lower social mobility.

83
00:06:32.283 --> 00:06:37.304
Now it's your turn. Part one: What should governments do about inequality?

84
00:06:42.604 --> 00:06:49.201
Part two: Use the subjunctive in a recommendation, give two policies and one counter-argument.

85
00:06:55.501 --> 00:07:07.743
I would propose that public education be strengthened in rural areas and that taxation become more progressive. Critics might argue this discourages investment, but it is vital that every child have a fair start.

86
00:07:08.043 --> 00:07:10.414
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:11.417 --> 00:07:17.227
Great work today! You learned eight new words and subjunctive and formal structures (mastery).

88
00:07:17.527 --> 00:07:20.946
Today's value is kindness. Be kind to everyone.

89
00:07:21.246 --> 00:07:24.397
See you in Unit forty-nine. Goodbye!

90
00:07:24.697 --> 00:07:27.466
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

